{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HcSPzGoPMxbb9jurvDz884","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"ACL Rolling Review\" review form \"overall assessment\" \"1\" \"2\" \"3\" \"4\" \"5\" soundness excitement scale description 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \\\"Publish or Perish\\\" Circus | CSPaper Forum\",\"url\":\"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus\"},{\"title\":\"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/acguidelines.md at main · yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview/blob/main/acguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"}]\n\nI found detailed information about the ACL Rolling Review form with the soundness and excitement scales. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## ACL Rolling Review Scoring System (2025)\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness evaluation asks: \"Given that this is a short/long paper, is it sufficiently sound and thorough? Does it clearly state scientific claims and provide adequate support for them?\"\n\nThe scale includes:\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n### Excitement Scale\n\nThe question asks: \"How exciting is this paper for you? Excitement is subjective, and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field.\"\n\nThe scale includes:\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"bc23a3ed-54e6-4765-ad99-90d8b9c36542","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:03:00.766Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"ACL Rolling Review\" review form \"overall assessment\" \"1\" \"2\" \"3\" \"4\" \"5\" soundness excitement scale description 2025","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_019M2dkEbKHvDdWKUB761Gzq","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"🎪 ACL 2025 Reviews Are Out: To Rebut or Flee? The Harsh Reality of NLP’s \"Publish or Perish\" Circus | CSPaper Forum","url":"https://forum.cspaper.org/topic/27/acl-2025-reviews-are-out-to-rebut-or-flee-the-harsh-reality-of-nlp-s-publish-or-perish-circus"},{"title":"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023","url":"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/acguidelines.md at main · yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/yoshitomo-matsubara/aclrollingreview/blob/main/acguidelines.md"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"}]},"I found detailed information about the ACL Rolling Review form with the soundness and excitement scales. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## ACL Rolling Review Scoring System (2025)\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness evaluation asks: \"Given that this is a short/long paper, is it sufficiently sound and thorough? Does it clearly state scientific claims and provide adequate support for them?\"\n\nThe scale includes:\n- 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\n- 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential.\n- 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n### Excitement Scale\n\nThe question asks: \"How exciting is this paper for you? Excitement is subjective, and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field.\"\n\nThe scale includes:\n- 5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n- 1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community (e.g. it is in no way related to computational processing of language).\n\n### Overall Assessment\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":7.894932772}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Nf6TxeCvEyYDoyBfrpo7BZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the ARR review form. The scoring scales are:\n\n**Soundness** (1-5):\n- 5 = Excellent\n- 4 = Strong\n- 3 = Acceptable\n- 2 = Poor (likely)\n- 1 = Major Issues (likely)\n\n**Excitement** (1-5):\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Somewhat Exciting (likely)\n\n\n- 2 = Not Very Exciting (likely)\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment** (1-5):\n- 5 = Strong Accept\n- 4 = Accept\n- 3 = Borderline\n- 2 = Reject\n- 1 = Strong Reject\n\nNow I need to assign two reviewers for the full review process. The first reviewer will focus on methods and experiments while the second handles novelty and related work. The roundtable then proceeds through independent reviews, cross-sharing of feedback, convergence toward consensus, and a final step.\n\nI'm thinking through how to set up the parallel reviewer agents. They'll need the paper content, the review context YAML, and the scoring rubric to conduct independent evaluations. Since the PDF is already in our conversation, I could write the reviews directly, but using agents for each reviewer role would be more structured. The challenge is that the agents won't have direct access to the PDF, so I'll need to include the full paper text and context YAML in their prompts to give them everything they need to work independently.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1150,"cache_read_input_tokens":57668,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1150},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"a910a7af-8ded-4e4d-8fc6-2c0402fc8916"}
