{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Af1zJVeoj1nZGf8kSoCAht","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — Embedding Alignment Gap\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\tEMNLP 2026 ARR (deadline: May 25, 2026) — Findings full paper (8 pages)\n5\t\n6\t## Title (working)\n7\t\"The Embedding Alignment Gap: Why Diversity-Based Data Selection Accelerates Model Collapse in Iterative Self-Training\"\n8\t\n9\t## Core Insight\n10\tIn iterative self-training (student-as-generator feedback loop), the geometric diversity of a model's internal embeddings becomes systematically misaligned with content diversity (measured by rare n-gram survival). This \"alignment gap\" worsens monotonically across generations, causing diversity-based data selection using internal representations to **accelerate** rather than prevent model collapse. Frozen external embeddings (e.g., Contriever) are immune to this misalignment.\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims\n13\t\n14\t### C1: Alignment Gap Exists and Worsens Monotonically\n15\t- Internal embedding diversity selection ratio degrades from ~1.04 (Gen 0) to ~0.52 (Gen 7)\n16\t- The model increasingly selects vocabulary-poor samples when using its own representations for diversity scoring\n17\t- Evidence: Phase 2 RGDS selection diagnostics (GPT2-125M, WikiText-103, self-iterate)\n18\t\n19\t### C2-weak: Frozen External Embeddings Are Immune to the Gap\n20\t- Contriever-based selection maintains ratio >1.1 across all generations (1.14→1.28 through Gen 4)\n21\t- External embeddings improve model quality (PPL -6.5% vs uncurated) but do NOT improve rare n-gram survival\n22\t- This is a comparative claim: external embeddings \"don't make the mistake\" internal ones make, not a solution to vocabulary collapse\n23\t- The dissociation between PPL improvement and rare n-gram stagnation itself is an interesting finding\n24\t\n25\t### C3: The Gap Causes Accelerated Collapse\n26\t- RGDS (internal diversity selection) produces PPL 75.07 vs uncurated 50.28 (+49%) and rare n-gram 0.0051 vs 0.0090 (-43%)\n27\t- Complete causal chain: embedding space contracts → \"geometrically diverse\" = \"vocabulary-poor\" → selection amplifies homogeneity → accelerated collapse\n28\t\n29\t## Secondary Claims\n30\t\n31\t### S1: PPL and Rare N-gram Measure Different Collapse Dimensions\n32\t- External selection improves PPL (-6.5%) without improving rare n-gram survival\n33\t- Selection ratio >1.1 translates to PPL gains but not vocabulary diversity gains\n34\t- Implication: semantic diversity ≠ lexical diversity in the context of iterative distillation\n35\t\n36\t### S2: Representation Quality Metrics Don't Detect Diversity Collapse\n37\t- RankMe increases during collapse (280→303, +8%)\n38\t- k-NN probe accuracy stable (0.988 throughout)\n39\t- These metrics track representation structure, not content diversity — a cautionary finding for monitoring frameworks\n40\t\n41\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n42\t\n43\t### R1: \"GPT2-125M is too small — does this generalize?\"\n44\t- Response: GPT2-medium (355M) experiments planned; alignment gap mechanism (embedding space contraction under self-iterate) is architecture-general\n45\t- If GPT2-medium shows gap: \"scales beyond toy models\"\n46\t\n47\t### R2: \"Only one dataset (WikiText-103)\"\n48\t- Response: OpenWebText subset planned as P1\n49\t- The mechanism is data-agnostic (self-iterate collapse is well-documented across datasets)\n50\t\n51\t### R3: \"N=1 seed — is this noise?\"\n52\t- Response: 3 seeds per configuration planned; the monotonic trend over 8 generations (not a single-point measurement) reduces seed sensitivity concern\n53\t- seed=123 already running as replication\n54\t\n55\t### R4: \"You only found a problem, not a solution\"\n56\t- Response: (a) Embedding re-anchoring experiment planned as simple mitigation; (b) the finding itself has immediate practical value — practitioners should avoid internal-embedding-based curation during iterative training\n57\t- If re-anchoring works → upgrade to \"found + solved\"\n58\t\n59\t### R5: \"What's the theoretical explanation for the gap?\"\n60\t- Connect to neural collapse literature (Papyan et al. 2020): self-iterate reduces intra-class variability → embedding effective dimensionality drops → greedy submodular selection in low-dim space favors collapsed mode directions\n61\t- Not a formal proof, but a plausible mechanistic intuition with empirical support\n62\t\n63\t### R6: \"External embeddings don't improve rare n-gram — how is C2 useful?\"\n64\t- C2 is comparative: shows the gap is specific to internal embeddings under training feedback, not an inherent property of embedding-based selection\n65\t- PPL improvement shows external selection captures meaningful diversity (semantic level), even if not lexical level\n66\t- Opens future work: what selection signal DOES preserve lexical diversity?\n67\t\n68\t## Narrative Arc\n69\t1. **Setup**: Iterative self-training causes model collapse (established). Diversity-based data curation is a natural mitigation idea.\n70\t2. **Surprise**: Using the model's own representations for diversity selection makes collapse WORSE, not better.\n71\t3. **Explanation**: The Embedding Alignment Gap — internal representations' notion of \"diverse\" diverges from content diversity as training progresses.\n72\t4. **Contrast**: Frozen external embeddings don't suffer this misalignment (but also don't solve vocabulary collapse).\n73\t5. **Implication**: Embedding-based diversity selection is unreliable in iterative settings; the field needs vocabulary-aware alternatives.\n74\t\n75\t## Related Work Positioning\n76\t- Model collapse: Shumailov et al. (Nature 2024), Alemohammad et al. (2024), Dohmatob et al. (2024)\n77\t- Data selection/curation: SemDeDup (Abbas 2023), D4 (Tirumala 2023), DSIR (Xie 2023) — these use external embeddings for static (non-iterative) curation; we show iterative setting introduces new failure mode\n78\t- Neural collapse: Papyan et al. (2020) — theoretical grounding for why embeddings lose diversity signal\n79\t- Representation quality metrics: RankMe (Garrido et al. 2023) — we show RankMe doesn't detect diversity collapse\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0168gYhZs9amky2XRXtH6p6s","session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"bb730e12-e844-448f-8006-209481bc8b0b","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:05:40.440Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a74d8adf52d1413a7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nh5RBKXWYS5GCWfUn3wnYy","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","usage":{"total_tokens":129659,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":151821},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"dfdc9b69-3198-4e6a-9cd9-a9f0cbe30292","session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XEW4nvsvx7gkz3rw9qwcjE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FAPLAfNrjbx28aQcQV4AHY","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"phase-gated-compound-sparse","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260513T081019","overall_score":"borderline reject","report":"# Review Report: \"Embedding-Based Diversity Selection Neither Helps Nor Hurts Iterative Model Collapse: A Controlled Null Result\"\n\n**Reviewer:** R1 (Methods and Experiments focus)\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThis paper tests whether embedding-based diversity selection can slow model collapse in iterative self-training. Three strategies are compared across 8 self-training generations of GPT-2: uncurated random sampling, RGDS (greedy submodular selection using internal embeddings), and external selection using frozen Contriever embeddings. The central finding is a null result: no statistically significant difference between methods (p > 0.15) across 2 model scales, 2 datasets, and 3 random seeds. A secondary finding documents metric dissociation where RankMe increases while rare n-gram frequency decreases during collapse.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-controlled experimental design.** The three-method comparison is cleanly structured: internal embeddings (RGDS), frozen external embeddings (Contriever), and no selection (uncurated). This isolates the selection signal from the generation feedback loop. Restarting from the pretrained checkpoint each generation (rather than cumulative fine-tuning) is a defensible design choice that controls for compounding training artifacts.\n\n- **Comprehensive robustness coverage.** The paper systematically varies model scale (125M, 355M), dataset (WikiText-103, OpenWebText), and random seeds (3 for 125M, 2 for 355M). All 8 planned experiments from the experimental grid are reported. This is thorough for a workshop-length paper.\n\n- **Honest reporting of a null result.** The authors explicitly acknowledge their original hypothesis was wrong. The framing is honest rather than post-hoc rationalized, which is refreshing. The re-anchoring experiment (Section 4.5) is a well-designed diagnostic that strengthens the bottleneck interpretation.\n\n- **Metric dissociation finding is genuinely useful.** The observation that RankMe *increases* (281.5 to 291.2) while rare n-grams monotonically decrease is a clear and reproducible cautionary finding. This is actionable for practitioners monitoring iterative training pipelines.\n\n- **Clean presentation.** Tables are well-organized and the paper is concise. The writing does not oversell the contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### W1: Insufficient statistical power analysis for a null-result paper (Major)\n\nThe paper claims a null result based on p > 0.15 with N=3 seeds. For a null-result paper to be publishable, the community must trust the null -- this requires a formal power analysis showing the study had adequate power to detect a meaningful effect size. No such analysis is provided.\n\nWith N=3, the paired t-test has very low power for moderate effect sizes. Concretely: the cross-seed PPL standard deviation is ~0.55 (Table 4). To detect a 5% PPL excess (~2.4 points) at alpha=0.05 with 80% power using a paired t-test requires approximately N=5-6 seeds. With N=3, the study is substantially underpowered for effects smaller than ~10%. The current p-values (0.18, 0.31) are consistent with either true null *or* a real but underpowered effect. The paper cannot distinguish between these scenarios.\n\n**Recommendation:** Report a formal power analysis (e.g., minimum detectable effect size at 80% power given N=3 and observed variance). Alternatively, use equivalence testing (TOST procedure) to positively demonstrate that the methods are equivalent within a pre-specified margin.\n\n### W2: Selection ratio decline is non-trivial but dismissed (Major)\n\nThe RGDS selection ratio declines from 1.02 to 0.93 over 8 generations -- a 9% decline. The external selection ratio declines from 1.05 to 0.97 -- an 8% decline. The paper dismisses this as irrelevant because downstream metrics do not differ. But this dismissal is problematic:\n\n1. A 9% decline in selection ratio over only 8 generations suggests that over longer horizons (e.g., 20-50 generations), the ratio could degrade substantially further. The paper does not explore extrapolation.\n2. The fact that the re-anchoring experiment restores ratio to 0.99 without improving downstream metrics is evidence for the bottleneck interpretation, but the paper does not consider that the 9% ratio decline may simply be too small to produce detectable downstream effects. If the \"alignment gap\" hypothesis requires a ratio of ~0.5 to manifest in PPL, then 8 generations is insufficient to test it.\n3. There is a consistent ordering across all conditions: External PPL < Uncurated PPL < RGDS PPL. This ordering holds in every single cell of every table. A consistent ordering that fails to reach significance with N=3 is exactly what underpowered studies look like.\n\n### W3: Self-training protocol has non-standard elements that may suppress selection effects (Moderate)\n\nThe protocol fine-tunes from the *original pretrained checkpoint* each generation rather than from the previous generation's checkpoint. While this is stated clearly, it substantially weakens the feedback loop compared to the standard model collapse setup (Shumailov et al. 2024) where M_t is fine-tuned from M_{t-1}. With checkpoint resetting, each generation starts from the same initialization, meaning the model never fully \"forgets\" the original distribution. This could specifically suppress the embedding alignment gap: since the model is always starting from pretrained weights, its embedding space never drifts far enough to cause the hypothesized misalignment.\n\nThe paper should clarify whether this design choice was motivated by prior work or by practical considerations, and discuss whether cumulative fine-tuning might produce different results.\n\n### W4: The \"uncurated\" baseline uses random sampling, not the full pool (Moderate)\n\nThe uncurated condition samples k sequences from the N generated (matching the selection budget), not training on all N. This is appropriate for controlled comparison with the selection methods, but it means the uncurated baseline is itself a form of selection (random subsampling). If N is substantially larger than k, the random subsampling could itself introduce variance reduction effects. The selection_budget_ratio is listed as 0.5 in the code but not clearly stated in the paper. The ratio between N and k should be explicitly reported.\n\n### W5: Rare n-gram metric definition lacks sensitivity analysis (Moderate)\n\nThe rare n-gram metric uses a fixed threshold (4-grams appearing <=5 times in the original corpus). The paper provides no sensitivity analysis over this threshold. If the threshold were set to <=1 (truly rare) or <=20 (moderately rare), would the null result hold? Different thresholds capture different parts of the distributional tail, and the current threshold may fall in a regime where all methods produce essentially identical tail behavior.\n\n### W6: No downstream task evaluation (Minor)\n\nThe paper evaluates only PPL and rare n-gram frequency. Including even one downstream task (e.g., NLG diversity metrics like Self-BLEU, distinct-n, or MAUVE) would strengthen the null result claim. PPL alone can miss diversity changes that affect generation quality.\n\n### W7: Missing intermediate generations in cross-seed tables (Minor)\n\nTable 3 only reports Gen-7 endpoints for seeds 123 and 456, with intermediate generations only shown for seed 42 (Table 1). A null result paper should show that the trajectories are indistinguishable at *all* generations, not just the endpoint. The full trajectories for all seeds should at minimum be in an appendix.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Power analysis:** What is the minimum effect size (in PPL points or percentage) that your study could detect at 80% power with N=3 seeds? Would you consider running equivalence tests (TOST) to positively demonstrate equivalence?\n\n2. **Checkpoint resetting:** What motivated resetting to the pretrained checkpoint each generation? Have you verified that the same null result holds with cumulative fine-tuning (M_{t+1} trained from M_t)?\n\n3. **Selection budget:** What is the exact selection ratio (k/N)? If it is 0.5, have you tested more aggressive budgets (e.g., k/N = 0.1) where the selection signal would be stronger?\n\n4. **Longer horizons:** The selection ratio decline is ~1%/generation. If you extrapolated to 20+ generations, would you expect the ratio to eventually reach the ~0.5 level that might produce detectable downstream effects? Is 8 generations sufficient to test the alignment gap hypothesis?\n\n5. **Rare n-gram threshold:** How sensitive is the null result to the <=5 threshold? What happens at <=1 or <=20?\n\n6. **Generation quality:** You mention nucleus sampling (p=0.95, temp=1.0). Have you verified that the generated text is coherent? Could degenerate generations (where selection methods would differ more) be masked by high-temperature sampling that already produces high-variance output?\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n### Soundness: 2 (Borderline)\n\nThe experimental setup is clean and controlled, but the absence of a power analysis is a critical gap for a null-result paper. The consistent method ordering (External < Uncurated < RGDS) across all conditions, combined with N=3, suggests the study may be underpowered rather than truly null. The checkpoint-resetting protocol may also suppress the very effect being tested. The re-anchoring diagnostic and metric dissociation findings are methodologically sound.\n\n### Excitement: 2 (Not very exciting)\n\nA null result showing that diversity selection does not affect model collapse is mildly informative but not surprising given the bottleneck analysis. The metric dissociation finding (RankMe increasing during collapse) is the most novel contribution but is a secondary finding. The null result, even if fully trusted, does not substantially advance understanding of model collapse -- it rules out one mitigation but without explaining why.\n\n### Overall Assessment: 2 (Reject)\n\nThe paper is competent and honest, but does not clear the bar for EMNLP 2026 for several reasons: (1) The null result is not sufficiently powered to be trusted, and the consistent method ordering hints at a real but undetected effect. (2) The protocol design choices (checkpoint resetting, 8 generations) may specifically suppress the effect under study. (3) The metric dissociation secondary finding, while interesting, is insufficiently developed to carry the paper on its own. (4) The practical takeaway (\"selection doesn't help in iterative settings\") is narrow and may not generalize beyond this specific experimental configuration. For a null-result paper at a top venue, the standard is high: the community must be confident the null is real, and the null itself must be surprising enough to redirect future work. Neither condition is fully met here.\n\n### Confidence: 4 (Confident)\n\nI have reviewed the experimental code, data, and all supporting materials in detail. My assessment is grounded in specific numerical evidence from the paper. The main uncertainty is whether the checkpoint-resetting protocol constitutes a fundamental flaw or a reasonable design choice -- I lean toward flaw but acknowledge reasonable disagreement.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Recommendation\n\n**Reject** with encouragement to resubmit after: (1) adding formal power analysis or equivalence testing; (2) running the experiment with cumulative fine-tuning to verify the null holds under standard model collapse protocol; (3) extending to more seeds (N >= 5) or more generations (T >= 16) to address underpowering concerns; and (4) expanding the metric dissociation analysis, which is the most publishable component of this work.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1863,"cache_read_input_tokens":127409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1863,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nh5RBKXWYS5GCWfUn3wnYy","session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"713a876f-5ae4-4390-89db-d1f73c172021"}
