{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nh5RBKXWYS5GCWfUn3wnYy","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is the summary:\n\n---\n\n**Review delivered for the \"worst\" tier paper:** \"Embedding-Based Diversity Selection Neither Helps Nor Hurts Iterative Model Collapse: A Controlled Null Result\"\n\n**Scores:** Soundness 2, Excitement 2, Overall 2 (Reject), Confidence 4\n\n**Key findings in the review:**\n\n1. **Underpowered null result (Major).** With N=3 seeds and cross-seed std ~0.55 PPL, the study lacks power to detect moderate effect sizes (< 10%). No formal power analysis or equivalence testing (TOST) is provided, which is essential for a null-result paper to be trusted.\n\n2. **Consistent method ordering hints at real effect (Major).** External PPL < Uncurated PPL < RGDS PPL holds in every single experimental cell. This is exactly what an underpowered study of a real but small effect looks like. The selection ratio decline (1.02 to 0.93) is dismissed too quickly.\n\n3. **Checkpoint resetting may suppress the target effect (Moderate).** Fine-tuning from the pretrained checkpoint each generation (rather than cumulative M_t from M_{t-1}) prevents the embedding space from ever drifting far enough to test the alignment gap hypothesis properly.\n\n4. **Metric dissociation finding is the strongest contribution.** RankMe increasing during collapse while rare n-grams decrease is genuinely useful, but insufficiently developed to carry the paper alone.\n\n5. **Missing sensitivity analyses.** No variation of the rare n-gram threshold, no downstream task evaluation, and incomplete intermediate-generation reporting for non-primary seeds.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T081019/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a74d8adf52d1413a7 (use SendMessage with to: 'a74d8adf52d1413a7' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 132858\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 166082</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"b5a25250-043e-4505-894b-321b2ba6b62a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:06:42.244Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## ARR Scoring Rubric\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent (most thorough study), 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Borderline (some claims not well supported), 1=Poor (major issues)\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Somewhat, 2=Not very, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall Assessment** (1-5): 5=Strong Accept, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Embedding-Based Diversity Selection Neither Helps Nor Hurts Iterative Model Collapse: A Controlled Null Result\"\n\n**Abstract:** Iterative self-training where a language model is repeatedly fine-tuned on its own generations leads to model collapse. The authors test whether embedding-based diversity selection can slow this collapse by comparing 3 data selection strategies across 8 self-training generations: (1) uncurated random sampling, (2) RGDS (greedy submodular selection using internal embeddings), (3) external selection using frozen Contriever embeddings. Across 2 model scales (GPT-2 125M and 355M), 2 datasets (WikiText-103 and OpenWebText), and 3 random seeds, they find **no statistically significant difference** (p > 0.15). Gen-7 PPL differs by < 3%; rare n-gram survival rates are indistinguishable. Re-anchoring restores selection ratio without affecting collapse metrics. Secondary finding: metric dissociation (RankMe increases while rare n-gram frequency decreases).\n\n## Method Details (Section 3)\n\n**Self-Training Protocol:** T=8 generations. At gen t: Model M_t generates N samples (nucleus p=0.95, temp=1.0). Selection method chooses subset S_t of size k. Model M_{t+1} fine-tuned from *original pretrained checkpoint* on S_t.\n\n**Selection Methods:**\n- Uncurated: uniform random sampling (baseline)\n- RGDS (Internal): greedy submodular diversity using model's last-layer hidden states, facility-location objective\n- External (Contriever): same algorithm but frozen Contriever embeddings\n\n**Metrics:**\n- PPL on held-out validation using original pretrained model\n- Rare n-gram frequency (4-grams appearing ≤5 times in original corpus)\n- Selection ratio (rare n-gram in selected / full pool)\n- RankMe (effective rank of embedding covariance)\n- Rank correlation (Spearman between embedding diversity score and rare n-gram content)\n- k-NN accuracy\n\n## Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Main, GPT-2 125M, WikiText-103, seed=42):**\n| Gen | PPL_Uncur | PPL_RGDS | PPL_Ext | Rare_Uncur | Rare_RGDS | Rare_Ext |\n|-----|-----------|----------|---------|------------|-----------|----------|\n| 0   | 37.0      | 37.2     | 36.8    | .0290      | .0288     | .0291    |\n| 1   | 44.8      | 44.5     | 44.1    | .0193      | .0195     | .0196    |\n| 3   | 56.8      | 55.1     | 54.6    | .0117      | .0118     | .0119    |\n| 5   | 51.6      | 51.0     | 50.1    | .0098      | .0097     | .0098    |\n| 7   | 47.9      | 48.5     | 47.2    | .0087      | .0088     | .0089    |\n\nNon-monotonic PPL (peaks gen 3, partially recovers). Rare n-gram monotonically decreases. Inter-method difference negligible.\n\n**Table 2 (Gen-7 endpoints):**\n| Method | PPL | Rare | RankMe | RankCorr | SelRatio |\n|--------|-----|------|--------|----------|----------|\n| Uncurated | 47.9 | .0087 | 289.8 | -0.088 | — |\n| RGDS | 48.5 | .0088 | 291.2 | -0.082 | 0.93 |\n| External | 47.2 | .0089 | 290.5 | -0.079 | 0.97 |\n\n**Cross-seed (Table 3-4):** 3 seeds (42, 123, 456). 3-seed aggregate: RGDS mean PPL 48.53±0.55, Uncurated 48.00±0.55, External 47.23±0.46. Paired t-test: RGDS vs uncurated p=0.31, external vs uncurated p=0.18.\n\n**GPT-2 Medium 355M (Table 5):** Same pattern. RGDS 29.4, Uncurated 29.0, External 28.5. RGDS excess +1.4%. 2nd seed confirms (p=0.38).\n\n**OpenWebText (Table 6):** RGDS 38.2, Uncurated 37.8, External 37.3. RGDS excess +1.1%.\n\n**Re-anchoring (Table 7):** Restores selection ratio from 0.93→0.99 but PPL only improves 0.6% (48.5→48.2), rare n-gram unchanged (.0088). Shows bottleneck is candidate pool, not selection.\n\n**Metric dissociation (Sec 4.6):** RankMe increases 281.5→291.2 during collapse. k-NN stays 0.988. Rank correlation -0.023 to -0.091 (weak, negative).\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what the full plan includes)\nThe complete experiment plan includes:\n1. Phase 2 main comparison (3 methods × 8 gens, seed=42) ✓ in paper\n2. External_only seed=123 replication ✓ partially in paper\n3. 3 methods × seed=123 full replication ✓ in paper\n4. 3 methods × seed=456 full replication ✓ in paper  \n5. GPT2-medium 355M (seed=42) ✓ in paper\n6. GPT2-medium seed=123 ✓ in paper\n7. Re-anchoring experiment ✓ in paper\n8. OpenWebText cross-dataset ✓ in paper\n\nCoverage assessment: Paper covers all 8 planned experiments.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodology soundness**: Is the experimental protocol well-designed? Are there confounds? Is the self-training loop properly controlled?\n2. **Statistical analysis**: Is 3 seeds sufficient? Are the statistical tests appropriate? Is the power analysis adequate?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are the ablations sufficient? Missing experiments?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Are details sufficient for reproduction?\n5. **Metric choices**: Are PPL, rare n-gram, selection ratio the right metrics?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary of the paper (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with specific evidence)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall (1-5) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe critical and constructive. EMNLP acceptance rate ~22-25%. For a null result paper, the bar is: is it rigorous enough that the community can trust the null, and is the null itself surprising/informative enough?\n","agentId":"a74d8adf52d1413a7","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is the summary:\n\n---\n\n**Review delivered for the \"worst\" tier paper:** \"Embedding-Based Diversity Selection Neither Helps Nor Hurts Iterative Model Collapse: A Controlled Null Result\"\n\n**Scores:** Soundness 2, Excitement 2, Overall 2 (Reject), Confidence 4\n\n**Key findings in the review:**\n\n1. **Underpowered null result (Major).** With N=3 seeds and cross-seed std ~0.55 PPL, the study lacks power to detect moderate effect sizes (< 10%). No formal power analysis or equivalence testing (TOST) is provided, which is essential for a null-result paper to be trusted.\n\n2. **Consistent method ordering hints at real effect (Major).** External PPL < Uncurated PPL < RGDS PPL holds in every single experimental cell. This is exactly what an underpowered study of a real but small effect looks like. The selection ratio decline (1.02 to 0.93) is dismissed too quickly.\n\n3. **Checkpoint resetting may suppress the target effect (Moderate).** Fine-tuning from the pretrained checkpoint each generation (rather than cumulative M_t from M_{t-1}) prevents the embedding space from ever drifting far enough to test the alignment gap hypothesis properly.\n\n4. **Metric dissociation finding is the strongest contribution.** RankMe increasing during collapse while rare n-grams decrease is genuinely useful, but insufficiently developed to carry the paper alone.\n\n5. **Missing sensitivity analyses.** No variation of the rare n-gram threshold, no downstream task evaluation, and incomplete intermediate-generation reporting for non-primary seeds.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T081019/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":166082,"totalTokens":132858,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3135,"cache_read_input_tokens":129272,"output_tokens":450,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3135},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":450,"cache_read_input_tokens":129272,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3135,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3135,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a2f37771bf42bffe5","tool_use_id":"toolu_0168gYhZs9amky2XRXtH6p6s","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","usage":{"total_tokens":95170,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":170708},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"39e1db7b-e562-4f6e-a9e1-dce07e8a6360","session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018yLwg27QmGYU7t6ppeKXQ6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011o14EEyAqUrBMUFutJbtYk","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"phase-gated-compound-sparse","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260513T081019","overall_score":"borderline reject (2.0/5)","report":"# Reviewer R2 Report: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper**: \"Embedding-Based Diversity Selection Neither Helps Nor Hurts Iterative Model Collapse: A Controlled Null Result\"\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 ARR\n**Reviewer Focus**: Novelty and Related Work\n**Date**: 2026-05-13\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper tests whether embedding-based diversity selection (internal RGDS or external Contriever) can modulate model collapse speed in iterative self-training. Across 2 GPT-2 scales, 2 datasets, and 3 seeds, all three selection methods produce statistically indistinguishable collapse trajectories (Gen-7 PPL within 3%, rare n-gram within noise, all paired p > 0.15). A re-anchoring intervention restores selection ratio without affecting collapse metrics, localizing the bottleneck to the generation side. A secondary finding documents metric dissociation (RankMe up while rare n-gram down during collapse).\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Honest and transparent reporting of a null result.** The paper clearly states that its original \"alignment gap\" hypothesis was wrong and pivots to reporting the null finding. This intellectual honesty is commendable and too rare in ML research. The narrative arc -- \"we hypothesized X, X was wrong, and here is why\" -- is well-constructed.\n\n- **Clean experimental design.** The three-way comparison (uncurated / internal RGDS / external Contriever) with matched selection budgets and the same submodular objective is methodologically sound. Varying only the embedding source while holding everything else constant is the right way to isolate the variable of interest.\n\n- **Appropriate statistical framing of null results.** The paper reports p-values, notes they are all above 0.15, and correctly interprets this as inability to reject the null hypothesis rather than claiming equivalence. The cross-seed consistency adds credibility.\n\n- **Re-anchoring experiment as diagnostic.** The re-anchoring intervention that restores selection ratio without affecting downstream metrics is the strongest piece of evidence in the paper. It provides causal evidence that the bottleneck is in generation, not selection. This analytical decomposition is the most valuable contribution.\n\n- **Cross-condition coverage.** Testing across 2 model scales, 2 datasets, and 3 seeds for the primary configuration demonstrates thoroughness. The null result is robust, which strengthens the claim.\n\n- **Metric dissociation finding (S2).** The observation that RankMe increases while rare n-gram decreases is a useful cautionary note for practitioners, though it requires substantially more analysis to be a standalone contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### 1. The null result is unsurprising from first principles (Major)\n\nThe core finding -- that selecting a diverse subset from an already-homogeneous candidate pool cannot recover diversity that was never generated -- is predictable from basic reasoning about the data generation pipeline. If the generator has lost the ability to produce rare tokens, no downstream selection method can conjure them. This is analogous to observing that sorting a bucket of identical balls by color yields no color diversity; the observation is true but not illuminating.\n\nThe paper's own framing acknowledges this: \"selection operates downstream of the diversity bottleneck.\" But this insight does not require 8 generations of self-training to derive -- it follows directly from the structure of the iterative loop. The experimental validation confirms the obvious rather than revealing something unexpected.\n\nA genuinely surprising null result would be one where there are good reasons to expect a difference. The paper frames its original hypothesis -- that internal selection would *accelerate* collapse via an \"alignment gap\" -- as the expected alternative. But this hypothesis was the authors' own construction. The community's prior expectation, based on first principles and the existing collapse literature (which emphasizes the feedback loop as the driver), would likely align with the null.\n\n### 2. Related work is severely underdeveloped (Major)\n\nOnly 10 references is far below EMNLP expectations for a paper engaging with model collapse and data selection, both of which are active research areas. Specific gaps:\n\n**Missing model collapse papers:**\n- Gerstgrasser et al. (2024) -- \"Is Model Collapse Inevitable?\" showed that accumulating data across generations (rather than replacing) avoids collapse. Directly relevant: their work demonstrates that the *data retention strategy* matters, which is precisely the class of intervention this paper tests.\n- Guo et al. (2024) -- \"Curious Decline of Linguistic Diversity\" documents vocabulary collapse specifically, highly relevant to the rare n-gram metric used here.\n- Seddik et al. (2024) -- theoretical analysis of collapse under Gaussian assumptions; relevant to understanding why selection cannot help when the generator distribution has already contracted.\n- Briesch et al. (2023) -- earlier empirical work on iterative training degradation.\n- Hataya et al. (2023) -- \"Will Large-scale Generative Models Corrupt Future Datasets?\" -- studies data contamination in the web-scale context.\n\n**Missing data selection literature:**\n- LESS (Xia et al., 2024) -- gradient-based data selection, a fundamentally different selection paradigm that might interact with collapse differently.\n- QuRating and other quality-based selection methods that operate on different principles than embedding diversity.\n- DPP-based selection (mentioned in limitations but not cited): Kulesza & Taskar (2012) is the foundational reference.\n- Facility-location submodular maximization: the paper uses this but does not cite foundational work (Iyer & Bilmes 2013, Wei et al. 2015, Kirchhoff & Bilmes 2014).\n\n**Missing conceptual connections:**\n- Concept drift / embedding drift: the core observation -- that internal representations become unreliable as the data distribution shifts -- is well-studied in continual learning and domain adaptation. The paper does not engage with this literature at all. Lu et al. (2019) surveys concept drift; Gama et al. (2014) covers change detection.\n- Active learning under distribution shift: the degradation of acquisition functions when the model's representation space shifts is known (Ash et al. 2020 BADGE, Kirsch et al. 2019). The alignment gap is essentially an acquisition function failure mode in a different guise.\n- Self-play and RLHF feedback loops: similar co-evolution dynamics occur in reinforcement learning from human feedback where the reward model co-evolves with the policy. The Goodhart's law / reward hacking connection is natural.\n- Representational collapse in contrastive learning: Jing et al. (2022) on dimensional collapse is directly relevant to the mechanism the paper speculates about.\n\n### 3. The \"alignment gap\" hypothesis lacks prior theoretical or empirical grounding (Major)\n\nThe paper frames its null result against the hypothesis that internal diversity selection would *accelerate* collapse. But this \"alignment gap\" hypothesis appears to be the authors' own construction without support from prior work. No citation is provided for why anyone should have expected embedding-based diversity selection to interact with collapse dynamics in the first place.\n\nThis matters because the value of a null result is proportional to how strongly the community believed the positive hypothesis. A null result refuting a well-established prediction is highly valuable (e.g., negative results in physics). A null result refuting the authors' own ad-hoc hypothesis is much less so -- it risks being a straw man argument.\n\nThe paper should either (a) provide evidence that the alignment gap hypothesis has been proposed or implied by prior work, or (b) reframe the contribution away from \"disproving our own hypothesis\" and toward \"systematic characterization of what doesn't work.\"\n\n### 4. Metric dissociation finding is shallow and incremental (Moderate)\n\nThe S2 finding (RankMe increases while content diversity decreases) is stated but not analyzed. Questions left unanswered:\n\n- *Why* does RankMe increase? The paper speculates about \"restructuring\" but provides no evidence. Is the increase in effective dimensionality driven by the representations spreading across directions orthogonal to content diversity? A singular value decomposition analysis, or tracking how the top-k principal components align with lexical diversity across generations, would transform this from an observation into an insight.\n- Is the RankMe increase specific to self-training, or does it occur in any continued fine-tuning? Without a control (e.g., fine-tuning on original real data), the paper cannot attribute this to collapse specifically.\n- The finding that embedding similarity does not correlate with surface-level lexical properties is well-known in the NLP/IR communities. The contribution is showing this in the model collapse context, which is incremental.\n\n### 5. Practical impact is unclear and potentially negligible (Moderate)\n\nThe paper warns practitioners not to use embedding-based diversity selection for iterative training. But:\n\n- Who is currently doing this? The paper does not cite any real-world pipeline that uses internal-embedding diversity selection in an iterative self-training loop. The warned-against practice may not exist in meaningful deployment.\n- The more interesting practical question -- whether *any* selection-side intervention can help -- is answered only for one narrow class of methods (greedy submodular with embedding diversity). Other approaches (gradient-based selection like LESS, reward-model-based filtering, simple heuristics like type-token ratio filtering) are not tested.\n- The paper's actionable recommendation (\"mitigation must act at the generation stage\") is too vague to be useful. This is equivalent to saying \"fix the problem at its source,\" which, while true, provides no concrete guidance.\n\n### 6. Only GPT-2 family -- generalization concerns are unaddressed (Moderate)\n\nBoth model scales (125M, 355M) are within the GPT-2 architecture family, with only a 2.8x parameter ratio. GPT-2 is a 2019 architecture with known limitations:\n\n- No attention improvements (flash attention, GQA, rotary embeddings)\n- No instruction tuning or alignment\n- Small vocabulary (50k BPE)\n\nThe null result might not hold for architectures where internal representations have different geometric properties. Pythia, LLaMA, or other modern architectures would provide meaningful diversity.\n\nThe limitations section mentions this but does not adequately address why readers should believe the result generalizes.\n\n### 7. Missing power analysis for the null result (Moderate)\n\nThe paper claims a null result with n=3 seeds. But:\n\n- What is the minimum detectable effect size given this sample? With n=3, the statistical power to detect a 5% PPL difference at alpha=0.05 is likely very low. The paper should report a power analysis.\n- Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. The paper conflates \"p > 0.15\" with \"no effect exists.\" A Bayesian approach (e.g., Bayes factor) or equivalence testing (TOST procedure) would be more appropriate for claiming a null result.\n- The paper should frame its finding as \"the effect, if it exists, is smaller than X% with 80% power\" rather than \"there is no effect.\"\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Prior expectation**: Can you cite any prior work that specifically predicts or implies that embedding-based diversity selection should modulate collapse speed? If not, why should the community find this null result surprising?\n\n2. **Power analysis**: Given n=3 seeds, what is the minimum effect size your design can detect at 80% power? Have you computed equivalence bounds?\n\n3. **Alternative selection methods**: Have you tested any non-embedding-based selection methods (e.g., type-token ratio filtering, perplexity-based selection, gradient-based selection)? These would help distinguish \"selection in general doesn't help\" from \"embedding-based selection doesn't help.\"\n\n4. **Gerstgrasser et al. (2024)**: Their work shows that accumulating data across generations avoids collapse. How does your result relate? If data *retention* strategy matters but data *selection* strategy does not, what does this tell us about the information-theoretic bottleneck?\n\n5. **RankMe mechanism**: Can you provide any analysis of *which* directions in embedding space drive the RankMe increase? Even a qualitative examination (e.g., do the top-3 principal components before and after collapse differ?) would help.\n\n6. **Would a trivial baseline work?** If you selected samples based on a simple surface-level diversity metric (e.g., unique token count, type-token ratio), would this also show a null result? This would distinguish whether the bottleneck is truly in generation vs. whether embedding-based metrics simply capture the wrong signal.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n**Soundness: 3/5 (Acceptable)**\nThe experimental design is clean and the statistical analysis is appropriate for what it is. The cross-condition robustness is a strength. However, the absence of a power analysis weakens the null result claim, and the metric dissociation finding is under-analyzed. The core methodology is sound but incomplete.\n\n**Excitement: 1.5/5 (Not exciting)**\nThe null result is unsurprising from first principles. The insight that downstream selection cannot compensate for upstream generation degradation is expected. The metric dissociation finding, while true, is incremental and under-analyzed. The paper does not change how the community would think about model collapse or data selection. A reader who understands the iterative collapse mechanism would correctly predict this result without running the experiments.\n\n**Overall: 2.0/5 (Reject)**\nThis paper is below the threshold for EMNLP main conference or Findings. The core null result, while well-executed experimentally, is predictable and does not advance the field's understanding of model collapse in a meaningful way. The related work is severely underdeveloped (10 references, missing entire subfields), the novelty is low, the practical impact is unclear, and the metric dissociation finding is too shallow to compensate. The paper reads as a well-done negative result from a pilot study rather than a standalone contribution. It would be more appropriate for a workshop (e.g., NeurIPS Negative Results Workshop, or a model collapse workshop) where the experimental grid could serve as a useful data point.\n\nFor the paper to reach Findings quality, it would need: (i) substantially expanded related work (25+ references), (ii) a power analysis and equivalence testing, (iii) testing at least one non-embedding-based selection method to broaden the claim, (iv) deeper analysis of the RankMe dissociation, and (v) connection to the concept drift and active learning literatures to properly contextualize the finding.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**\nI am familiar with the model collapse, data selection, and representation learning literatures. The main source of uncertainty is whether larger-scale models might exhibit different dynamics, but this does not affect my assessment of the paper as written.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Missing References\n\n| Reference | Relevance |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| Gerstgrasser et al. 2024 (\"Is Model Collapse Inevitable?\") | Data accumulation avoids collapse; directly relevant to data strategy |\n| Guo et al. 2024 (\"Curious Decline\") | Vocabulary collapse in LLMs; same metric focus (rare tokens) |\n| Seddik et al. 2024 | Theoretical analysis of collapse; relevant to understanding the bottleneck |\n| Briesch et al. 2023 | Earlier iterative training degradation work |\n| Hataya et al. 2023 | Data contamination at scale |\n| Xia et al. 2024 (LESS) | Gradient-based selection; alternative to embedding-based |\n| Kulesza & Taskar 2012 (DPPs) | Foundational reference for diversity selection |\n| Iyer & Bilmes 2013 / Wei et al. 2015 | Submodular selection theory (used but not cited) |\n| Ash et al. 2020 (BADGE) | Active learning acquisition function degradation |\n| Kirsch et al. 2019 | Active learning under distribution shift |\n| Jing et al. 2022 | Dimensional collapse in representations |\n| Lu et al. 2019 / Gama et al. 2014 | Concept drift surveys |\n\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12880,"cache_read_input_tokens":81757,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0168gYhZs9amky2XRXtH6p6s","session_id":"2df59a54-ef24-4a14-a88b-11610600ad72","uuid":"2e174ba5-5e5c-43bb-b3b4-7f15cfa6af3b"}
